# Judge item J0547

Does the finding describe the labelled defect? `describes`, `partially`, or `does-not-describe`.

## Finding

``` text
head/.claude/scripts/model-health-probe.sh:1475 — `_probe_configured` validates `<endpoint>/models` with `endpoint_validator__check --allowlist` (and `_curl_json` re-validates through `endpoint_validator__guarded_curl` with the same allowlist), so a configured https provider whose host is not in `lib/allowlists/loa-providers.json` (the Azure example in `.loa.config.yaml.example`, any hosted open-weight API) is refused with ENDPOINT-NOT-ALLOWED and reported UNKNOWN/endpoint_refused. The cheval path admits an operator-declared https host via `validate_declared_endpoint`, so `--provider <name>` only works for opted-in loopback/RFC1918 providers; the help text and D-7.5 docs promise any configured provider, and the "one endpoint rule" claim does not hold for the probe.
head/.claude/scripts/model-health-probe.sh:1578 — the probe has no `hounfour.locality` handling at all (no read of the key, no refusal), yet `scripts-reference.md`, `multi-model-reference.md`, `.loa.config.yaml.example` and the README all state that `model-health-probe.sh` refuses a hosted probe under `local_voices`. Under `local_voices` the probe still sends requests to every hosted built-in provider. Either implement the refusal or drop the claim from the four documents.
head/.claude/loa/reference/scripts-reference.md:73 — documents a `local_voices` refusal for `model-health-probe.sh --provider <name>` that the script does not implement (see the probe finding above); the row describes behaviour that does not exis …
```

